Incision and Drainage of Simple Abscess
General Practice Other
Fluctuant erythematous nodule, Exquisite localized pain, Purulent discharge upon incision, Staphylococcal culture

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-019
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing pain and systemic malaise secondary to a rapidly expanding, pointing subcutaneous abscess.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with a rapidly expanding, exquisitely painful subcutaneous mass on the posterior thigh, accompanied by a new-onset fever and an inability to sit or lie comfortably. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to perform an immediate surgical incision and drainage to relieve the extreme tissue tension, evacuate the purulent cavity, and prevent spontaneous, uncontrolled rupture or the onset of systemic sepsis. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to unmanageable pain and a significantly increased risk of systemic bacterial dissemination overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "There is a massive, boiling hot boil on the back of my leg, the pain is blinding, and I feel like I have the flu."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an exquisitely painful, erythematous, and warm subcutaneous nodule on the posterior right thigh. Over the past four days, there has been a rapid progressive enlargement of the lesion, culminating today in a highly fluctuant central area indicating suppuration and pointing. The patient reports severe induration of the surrounding skin tissue and describes the pain as a relentless, severe throbbing that completely prevents sitting or sleeping. The patient has developed a low-grade fever and mild systemic malaise over the last 12 hours.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely restricted by pain, unable to sit or lie supine.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of systemic sepsis if source control is not achieved immediately.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 38.0 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 92 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, diaphoretic, pacing the room because they cannot sit down.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the right posterior thigh reveals a 6cm x 6cm intensely erythematous, indurated mass with a 3cm central area of profound fluctuance and impending spontaneous rupture (pointing). Exquisite localized pain is elicited upon the lightest palpation. The presentation necessitates the infiltration of local anaesthetic around the perimeter, followed by a sharp incision using a scalpel yielding immediate purulent discharge. Expected investigation results include the isolation of Staphylococcus aureus, particularly methicillin-resistant strains, on bacterial culture of the purulent exudate.
Environmental Observations: Safe, brightly lit environment suitable for minor surgical intervention.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen taken with zero relief; verified.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Incision and Drainage of Simple Abscess (Posterior Thigh) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a localized, highly fluctuant, superficial mass with central pointing and purulent discharge, rather than generalized unilateral limb swelling and deep calf pain.

Uncomplicated Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The massive accumulation of localized, fluctuant pus requiring mechanical drainage, distinguishing it from a flat, spreading infection of the dermis alone.

Sebaceous Cyst (Uninfected) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of fever, extreme hyperalgesia, rapid expansion, and surrounding induration indicating an active, acute infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Infection Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient developed a large, acutely infected, fluctuant subcutaneous abscess causing extreme localized pain and early systemic symptoms (fever, tachycardia). Telephone triage accurately identified the absolute necessity for immediate surgical source control. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to perform an incision and drainage. The dense accumulation of purulent material under high pressure cannot be penetrated by systemic antibiotics alone; definitive treatment requires mechanical evacuation. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have resulted in an unacceptably prolonged period of agonizing pain, potential spontaneous messy rupture, and a high probability of the localized infection progressing to overt systemic sepsis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Infiltrated 1% Lidocaine around the perimeter (field block). Performed a sharp linear incision using a #11 scalpel, yielding approximately 30mL of immediate, foul-smelling purulent discharge. Performed blunt mechanical disruption of internal loculations using hemostats. Packed the resulting cavity with sterile ribbon gauze to promote healing by secondary intention. Collected a wound swab for M/C/S. Prescribed oral Cephalexin 500mg QID for 7 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote safe healing from the inside out and prevent the cavity from sealing over prematurely, strictly attend your scheduled daily dressing changes. Pack your car keys inside the bag of spare dressing supplies tonight. This physical barrier ensures that you cannot leave the house tomorrow without consciously handling your wound care materials, reinforcing the daily habit of attending your nursing review.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sterile dressings and tape.
Follow-up: Urgent GP or practice nurse review strictly within 24 hours for removal of the initial packing, reassessment, and repacking.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the Emergency Department immediately if your fever spikes violently with uncontrollable shaking (rigors), if the redness rapidly spreads down your leg, or if you become confused or dizzy.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The extreme throbbing pressure is instantly relieved. The wound must heal from the bottom up, which requires daily packing changes by a nurse for the next week.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the wound begins bleeding heavily through the thick dressing, or if the severe throbbing pain returns tomorrow, suggesting a new pocket of pus has formed.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The antibiotics alone could not reach the bacteria trapped inside the cavity. We had to physically cut it open and drain the pus. The packing ribbon keeps the cut open so it doesn't heal over the top and trap more infection inside.
Actionable Steps: Do not touch or remove the ribbon packing yourself. Keep the outer dressing completely dry. Attend your nursing appointment tomorrow without fail.

